{
  "dom_id": "step-q183371",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$(3n)!>{8}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case {{field-1}}.\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}>{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$\n{{field-4}},\nwe have\n\n$$\n{{field-5}}>{{field-6}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $(3n)!>{8}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2.$",
  "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n(3n)!>{8}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n\\ge 2,$ the base case is $P(2).$ So, we substitute $n=2$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n(3\\cdot 2)!>{8}^{2}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 2.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(3k)!>{8}^{k}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(3(k+1))!>{8}^{k+1}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $(3n)!>{8}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2.$",
